#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# nest

#% text
# `nest(f, x, n)` gives `f` applied `n` times to `x`: `f(f(...f(x)...))`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `n` must be a non-negative integer; `nest(f, x, 0)` is `x`.

#% item
# The intermediate values are not kept; `nest_list` gives them all.

#% item
# To iterate until the value stops changing, use `fix_point`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
nest(f, x, 3)
#% output
#= f(f(f(x)))

#% cell
nest(x => 2x, 1, 10)
#% output
#= 1024

#% cell
nest(x => x^2, y, 3)
#% output
#= y^8

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Exact rational arithmetic throughout:

#% cell
nest(x => (x + 2/x)/2, 1, 4)
#% output
#= 665857/470832

#% text
# A list as the state:

#% cell
nest(p => [p[2], p[1] + p[2]], [0, 1], 20)
#% output
#= [6765, 10946]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Newton's iteration for the square root of 2, at machine precision:

#% cell
nest(x => (x + 2/x)/2, 1.0, 6)
#% output
#= 1.414213562373095

#% text
# A continued fraction approaching the golden ratio:

#% cell
nest(x => 1 + 1/x, 1, 10)
#% output
#= 144/89

#% cell
n(nest(x => 1 + 1/x, 1, 10))
#% output
#= 1.6179775280898876

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `nest` is the last element of `nest_list`:

#% cell
nest(x => 3x + 1, 0, 5) == last(nest_list(x => 3x + 1, 0, 5))
#% output
#= true

#% text
# `fix_point` iterates until nothing changes:

#% cell
fix_point(x => (x + 2/x)/2, 1.0)
#% output
#= 1.414213562373095

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The count must be a non-negative integer:

#% cell
nest(f, x, -1)
#% output
#! failure(argument, nest, 3)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `nest_list`, `fix_point`, `fold`, `table`
